  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14173512/assets/2d2a4e7a727bd5643187203bcfc30e4cc8369e1e1fe53eea4d11b50cfb405622.png)\n\nIn the diagram shown above, $m∠1={58}^{∘}$ and $m∠4={122}^{∘}.$ Determine the value of $m∠3-m∠2.$",
  "worked_solution": "We have two pairs of vertical angles, and we know that vertical angles are congruent and therefore have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1 & =m∠2, \\\\ m∠3 & =m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $m∠1={58}^{∘}$ and $m∠4={122}^{∘},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{58}^{∘} & =m∠2, \\\\ m∠3 & ={122}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠3-m∠2 & ={122}^{∘}-{58}^{∘} \\\\  & ={64}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
